['Here is a simple implementation of `atoi()` and how you can use command line arguments in C. Please note that this is a simple implementation, for production-grade code, you might need to add error checking and handle edge cases properly.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int my_atoi(const char *str) {
    int res = 0;
    while (*str) {
        if (*str >= '0' && *str <= '9') {
            res = res*10 + (*str - '0'); 
        } else {
            break;
        }
        ++str;
    }
    return res;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {

    if (argc > 1) {
        printf("Converting string to integer: %s\n", argv[1]);
        int num = my_atoi(argv[1]);  // Convert the second command line argument to an integer.
        printf("Result: %d\n", num);
    } else {
        printf("No argument was passed.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```
To compile and run this code, you can use a terminal and type the following commands (assuming your file is named `main.c`):
```bash
gcc main.c -o main
./main 12345
```
This will convert the string "12345" to integer 12345. If you run it without any arguments, or with a non-numeric string as an argument, `my_atoi()` will return 0 because of how it is implemented - it only processes digits and stops at the first non-digit character.
']

