['Here is C code that will generate three random integers larger than 1000 and print them, their sum, and their sum using a long long. Note that this code uses the srand() function to seed the rand() function with the current time in order to get different numbers each time you run it.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

int main(){
    // Seed random number generator with current time
    srand(time(0));

    int a = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // Generate random integer between 1000 and 10000
    int b = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // Generate another random integer between 1000 and 10000
    int c = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // Generate the third random integer between 1000 and 10000

    printf("a : %d ", a); // Print the first number
    printf("b : %d ", b); // Print the second number
    printf("c : %d \n", c); // Print the third number

    int sum = a + b + c; // Calculate their sum as an integer
    long long sum2 = (long long)a + b + c; // Calculate their sum as a long long

    printf("sum : %d \n", sum); // Print the sum as an integer
    printf("sum2: %lld\n", sum2); // Print the sum as a long long

  return 0;
}
```
Just run this code and you should get three random integers, their sum and their sum as a long long. However, keep in mind that due to its nature of generating pseudo-random numbers, it's not guaranteed to generate every possible combination of digits each time you run the program. This is why if you run it multiple times, you might sometimes get duplicate values or small differences from one run to another.
']

