Also note that `trim_trailing_whitespace()` does not allocate any new memory for the trimmed string. As such, if there was a trailing whitespace, the original memory location will have its contents modified to reflect this change (which is why it is important to copy `argv[1]`). If you want to preserve the original string and return the trimmed version from the function call, you would need to allocate new memory for the trimmed string.
