['Below is a basic example of how you might implement such a function in C using libonion, a lightweight web library for creating HTTP servers. This code assumes that you have already set up your server correctly and are calling this function when a GET or POST request is made to /hello.

```c
#include <onion/log.h>
#include <onion/onion.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static int http_hello_page(void *p, onion_request *req, onion_response *res){
  if (onion_request_get_flags(req)&OR_HEAD){ // If this is a HEAD request
    return OCS_PROCESSED;
  }

  // Read the username from the POST data
  onion_dict *post = onion_request_get_post_dict(req);
  char *username = onion_dict_get_str(post, "username");
  if(!username){
    ONS_ERROR("No username provided in request body.");
    return OCS_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR; // Returns a 500 Internal Server Error.
  }

  // Construct the response body
  char outputbuf[128];
  sprintf(outputbuf, "Hello %s", username);

  // Write the response body to the response stream
  onion_response_write_headers(res);
  onion_response_printf(res, "%s", outputbuf);

  // Close the response stream
  onion_response_write0(res);

  return OCS_PROCESSED;
}
```
This code is a simple example and does not include all error checking that you would need for production-quality code. Also, it assumes that the username will be sent as part of the POST data, which should be properly encoded in your actual request. 

The function http_hello_page is called when the server receives a GET or POST request to the URL /hello. It reads the request body (the username) into the username variable and constructs the response body (the HTML page) into the outputbuf variable. Then it writes the response body to the response stream, closes the response stream, and returns OCS_PROCESSED, which tells the server that this function has handled the request.
