['Here's how you could implement this in C. Note, however, that this requires proper handling of file I/O which might not be straightforward or efficient when using standard C libraries for network programming, but it serves as an example of what the code might look like in such a case:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

// Assuming escape_html is implemented elsewhere and escapes '<' to "&lt;" etc.
char* escape_html(const char *input);

void http_hello_page(FILE *request, FILE *response) {
    // Buffer for reading username from request
    char username[1024];  // Assuming usernames are no longer than this
    if (fgets(username, sizeof(username), request)) {
        // Remove trailing newline character
        size_t len = strlen(username);
        if (username[len-1] == '\n')
            username[--len] = 0;

        // Escape HTML to avoid injection attacks
        char *escaped_username = escape_html(username);

        // Construct response
        fprintf(response, "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: text/plain\r\n\r\n");
        fprintf(response, "Hello %s", escaped_username);

        // Don't forget to free the memory allocated by escape_html() if it was dynamically allocated
        free(escaped_username);
    } else {
        // If reading from request failed, send a 400 Bad Request response
        fprintf(response, "HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request\r\nContent-Type: text/plain\r\n\r\n");
        fputs("Error reading username", response);
    }
}
```

Please note that this is a simplified example and may not cover all the requirements of your actual problem, such as dealing with multiple lines in request or error checking when writing to response. Also, be aware that the function escape_html() isn't provided here; you should implement it yourself or find an appropriate library to use.

Also remember that this is a simplified example and is not suitable for a real-world HTTP server. It assumes that the entire request can be read into memory at once (which may not always be true). Real-world servers typically use buffered input/output, so you would need to adjust your approach accordingly. Additionally, error checking and response formatting are simplified too - in a real-world application, this should be more robust and considerate of different HTTP versions.
